# The bare domain. Workers Assets runs with html_handling "none" -- every link
# on this site is written out in full ("/pages/cart.html") and the default
# would 307 each of them to an extensionless URL -- but that also means "/"
# matches no file on its own. 200 is a rewrite, not a redirect: the visitor
# keeps the "/" URL and gets the prerendered home page.
/    /index.html    200

# /pages/jenjum.html was one artist written into markup. Artists are rows
# now and share one page, so the old address is a permanent move rather
# than a dead link -- it has been in the sitemap, and it is what anything
# already printed or linked points at.
/pages/jenjum.html    /pages/artist-profile.html?id=jenjum-gadi    301

# --- The pictures are AVIF now ------------------------------------------
#
# public/assets/images ships .avif and nothing else: the PNG and JPEG
# sources were deleted once every twin existed, which took the directory
# from 41MB to 2.4MB. Their NAMES did not go anywhere. "/assets/images/
# hero.jpg" is written into markup, into the content modules, into rows in
# D1 that the shop edits from the panel, and into links other people have
# already saved — 215 references that no build step can rewrite, and some
# that no build step can even see.
#
# So the old name goes on working. 200 is a rewrite, not a redirect: the
# visitor keeps the URL they asked for and receives the AVIF, served with
# its own Content-Type, in one round trip and with no Worker invocation.
#
# shared/content/picture.js resolves the paths it can (deliverySrc) so the
# markup names the real file where it knows it. These two lines are what
# covers everything else — and they take precedence even when a source is
# still on disk, so a stray .png put back by hand cannot quietly start
# serving 2MB again.
/assets/images/:name.png    /assets/images/:name.avif    200
/assets/images/:name.jpg    /assets/images/:name.avif    200
